    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "I think the point here is that the pinned memory can also be safely used directly from the GPU. So it's legitimate to want to wrap up the pinned pointer into a cp.ndarray.\n\nThis function works:\n\n```\ndef from_numpy_pinned(x: np.ndarray, order='C') -> cp.ndarray:\n    assert order in 'FC' # TODO: support K\n    memptr = cupy.cuda.alloc_pinned_memory(x.nbytes)\n    memptr_ndarray = np.ndarray(x.shape, dtype=x.dtype, buffer=memptr, order=order)\n    memptr_ndarray[:] = x\n\n    # cupy rejects this simple approach (https://github.com/cupy/cupy/issues/8697):\n    #return cupy.ndarray(x.shape, dtype=x.dtype, memptr=memptr, order=order)\n\n    # We instead have to treat the memory as unowned to sneak it into a cupy array\n    mem_unowned = cupy.cuda.UnownedMemory(ptr=memptr.mem.ptr, size=memptr.mem.size, device_id=cupy.cuda.runtime.getDevice(), owner=memptr.mem)\n    memptr_unowned = cupy.cuda.MemoryPointer(mem_unowned, offset=0)\n    return cupy.ndarray(x.shape, dtype=x.dtype, memptr=memptr_unowned, order=order)\n```\n\nI think this avoids leaking the memory because `memptr.mem` is a `cupy.cuda.pinned_memory.PinnedMemory` object, which has. `__del__` method that deallocates the pinned memory.",
